Object.assign(window.deepArticles, {
  "american-spacecraft-manufacturing-industry": americanOrbitalDoctrineArticle({
    title:"American spacecraft-manufacturing industry",
    eyebrow:"United States · airframe houses, systems suppliers, and orbital yards",
    infoboxKicker:"National aerospace-industrial sector",
    lead:"The American spacecraft-manufacturing industry is the network of aircraft companies, propulsion manufacturers, electronics firms, federal laboratories, yards, and specialist suppliers that designs and builds American orbital vehicles. It remained institutionally separate from the companies that operated scheduled spacecraft. The Aerospace Force and federal laboratories defined early requirements and financed high-risk research, while competing private firms designed and manufactured the resulting machines. After 1985, commercial operators and other private customers became independent sources of spacecraft requirements.",
    canon:"The persistence of competing private design houses, separation between manufacturers and operators, participation of the named aviation firms, growing importance of company-funded designs after 1985, survival of terrestrial precision manufacturing, expansion of orbital assembly, and commercial predominance after 1990 are established. Exact prime-contractor assignments, corporate mergers, internal project numbers, factories, workforces, production totals, and 2012 ownership remain open.",
    sections:[
      {id:"scope",title:"Scope",html:`<p>The industry includes complete-vehicle prime contractors, propulsion houses, avionics and sensor firms, structural fabricators, thermal-system suppliers, life-support companies, federal research establishments, test ranges, and orbital yards. A company can belong to several categories through separate divisions.</p><p>Routine flight operations belong to carriers such as <a href='frontier-orbital-lines.html'>Frontier Orbital Lines</a>. Frontier specifies, maintains, modifies, and eventually sponsors vehicles, but its principal business is transportation rather than clean-sheet airframe manufacture.${americanOrbitalDoctrineCanon}</p>`},
      {id:"public-procurement",title:"Federal procurement era",html:`<p>The first runway-to-orbit generation followed the aircraft-procurement sequence. Federal laboratories performed basic research, the Aerospace Force defined a mission requirement, several firms submitted proposals, and the accepted machine received an O-series designation. Government test centers, ranges, traffic authorities, and operational commands remained closely involved after production began.</p><p>Pathfinder, Columbia, Liberty, Hercules, Ranger, Enterprise, Zenith, and the early O-Hundreds were therefore public programs built by private industrial organizations rather than products of a single federal design bureau. This arrangement distributed technical knowledge among firms: an unsuccessful bidder could retain lifting-body, thermal, propulsion, or systems experience and offer a different solution in a later competition.</p><p>The O-designation identified federal mission acceptance, not the organization that had conceived every component. Company proposal numbers, subcontractor marks, and test-article identities existed beneath the public service name, but did not usually dominate contemporary reporting before the commercial transition.</p>`},
      {id:"houses",title:"Principal design houses",html:"<div class='table-wrap'><table class='wiki-table'><thead><tr><th>Organization or lineage</th><th>Registered place in the spacecraft sector</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><a href='boeing-aircraft.html'>Boeing</a></td><td>Competing complete-vehicle and large-structure design house</td></tr><tr><td><a href='north-american-aviation.html'>North American / Rockwell</a></td><td>High-speed aircraft and reusable-aerospace lineage; complete-vehicle competition</td></tr><tr><td><a href='lockheed.html'>Lockheed</a></td><td>Advanced aircraft, reconnaissance, structures, and low-volume systems</td></tr><tr><td><a href='northrop.html'>Northrop</a></td><td>Lifting-body and control-system participation</td></tr><tr><td><a href='grumman.html'>Grumman</a></td><td>Crewed vehicles, docking, servicing, and maintainable structures</td></tr><tr><td><a href='mcdonnell-aircraft.html'>McDonnell Douglas</a></td><td>Transport, modular infrastructure, and orbital logistics competition</td></tr><tr><td><a href='martin-aircraft.html'>Martin</a></td><td>Lifting-body, utility-vehicle, and transition-system work</td></tr><tr><td><a href='general-dynamics-aerospace.html'>Convair / General Dynamics</a></td><td>Cryogenic structures, boosters, and unconventional large vehicles</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>The table identifies participating lineages, not exclusive specialties or fixed contractor awards. Exact post-divergence mergers and the allocation of individual O-Hundred programs remain open.</p>"},
      {id:"suppliers",title:"Propulsion and systems suppliers",html:`<p>Rocketdyne, Pratt & Whitney, Aerojet-General, Westinghouse, Hughes, General Electric, Curtiss-Wright, and Allison supplied propulsion, power, controls, pumps, electronics, thermal systems, and specialized machinery. These firms could serve several competing prime contractors on different programs.</p><p>The <a href='american-space-propulsion-industry.html'>space-propulsion sector</a> retained its own divisions of labor. No complete-vehicle manufacturer automatically controlled the engines, reactors, generators, guidance equipment, or life-support systems installed in its design.</p>`},
      {id:"division",title:"Terrestrial and orbital division of labor",html:"<p>Earth factories continued to make reactor fuel, turbopumps, bearings, valves, precision injectors, electronics, sensors, ion optics, high-grade alloys, pressure vessels, and other tightly controlled parts. Existing rail, road, power, metallurgy, machine-tool, and inspection networks remained essential.</p><p>Orbital yards joined trusses, tanks, radiators, cargo structures, and large propulsion assemblies that did not have to fit a terrestrial fairing or survive launch as one object. Components moved upward for final assembly, testing, repair, and integration.</p><p>The division was economic as well as technical. Launch mass favored finishing bulky low-density structures in orbit, whereas Earth retained the larger labor pool, chemical industry, quality-control institutions, and machines needed for precision production. An orbit-built ship was therefore not independent of terrestrial industry; it embodied a longer supply chain whose last assembly stage happened above the atmosphere.</p>"},
      {id:"process-control",title:"Process control and acceptance",html:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-s-20260816-w7n3c writer=s kind=technical created=2026-08-16T12:23:50-06:00 --><p>Flight hardware retained traceability from raw material to installed assembly. Records linked alloy heats, forgings, pressure-vessel lots, electronic batches, welds and bonds, heat treatment, cleaning, inspection, software version, calibrated tools, serialized parts, and nonconformance decisions to the finished vehicle. Propellant, oxygen, life-support, optical, and high-voltage equipment required different cleanliness and handling controls. A component that met dimensions could still be rejected for contamination, damaged surface finish, incorrect process history, or an undocumented repair.</p><p>Design qualification and flight-unit acceptance served different purposes. Qualification articles demonstrated that a design and its margins could withstand representative pressure, vibration, acoustic load, thermal vacuum, radiation, electromagnetic, duty-cycle, and fault conditions. Acceptance testing screened the workmanship and configuration of each delivered unit without consuming an excessive share of its service life. Dimensional inspection, nondestructive examination, proof and leak tests, electrical continuity and insulation checks, functional cycling, calibration, software and hardware-in-the-loop tests, and end-to-end command and telemetry checks were selected according to the failure consequences and access available after installation.</p><p>Orbital assembly moved the final control point rather than removing it. Yard crews verified receiving condition after launch, maintained clean and controlled work zones, surveyed structural and docking alignment, inspected joints, leak-checked pressure and fluid boundaries, checked cable and pipe routing, loaded the approved software configuration, and tested interfaces through simulators or connected systems. Integrated checkout established mass properties, power and thermal behavior, communications paths, guidance references, isolation boundaries, and abort or safe modes before release. Open discrepancies remained tied to an engineering disposition and operating restriction; a signed delivery certificate did not make an unresolved defect disappear.</p><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->"},
      {id:"customers",title:"Commercial customers after 1985",html:`<p>The <a href='commercial-orbital-transportation-act.html'>Commercial Orbital Transportation Act</a> created licensed operators able to acquire complete government-derived vehicles. Carriers, communications companies, station operators, mining concerns, and industrial consortia then began issuing requirements of their own.</p><p>These customers did not reproduce the military mission list. They asked for dispatch reliability, low crew burden, standardized cargo interfaces, long inspection intervals, cheap propellant handling, route endurance, and compatibility with particular stations or construction systems. Manufacturers consequently had reasons to develop automatic freighters, tugs, service craft, tankers, and passenger vehicles that no O-series requirement had anticipated.</p><p>By the 1990s manufacturers could finance a company project, build a demonstrator, and seek several customers before the Aerospace Force issued a service requirement. Company names and model numbers consequently became public identities beside federal designations.${americanOrbitalDoctrineCanon}</p>`},
      {id:"industrial-transition",title:"Industrial transition after the Open Door Depression",html:"<p>Federal austerity after 1990 threatened the later O-Hundred programs and reduced the predictable production blocks on which major contractors had relied. The programs survived in smaller or delayed form because they belonged to a military branch with unique deep-space responsibilities, but they no longer guaranteed enough work to organize the entire sector.</p><p>Manufacturers responded by spreading development risk. A design could combine federal research, company capital, a carrier's launch order, an industrial customer's specialized variant, and a later military purchase. This did not eliminate failure: demonstrators could lack customers, operators could consolidate, and proprietary interfaces could lose to a rival standard. It did mean that cancellation by one federal office no longer ended every technically mature project.</p><p>By the late 1990s the industry's routine output followed commercial traffic and construction, while federal contracts remained concentrated in combat, protected command, strategic patrol, and frontier exploration.</p>"},
      {id:"orbital-subsidiaries",title:"Orbital subsidiaries and yards",html:"<p>Manufacturers gradually established permanent orbital engineering, repair, and assembly organizations. These subsidiaries worked at Columbia, Liberty Yard, carrier annexes, depots, and later commercial construction sites. They remained dependent upon terrestrial corporate laboratories and factories.</p><p>The location of final assembly did not by itself identify the owner or designer. A yard could join a vehicle designed on Earth from parts made by several companies and financed by a carrier, consortium, or federal office.</p>"},
      {id:"status",title:"Industry by 2012",html:"<p>By 5 January 2012 the industry served military, transport, station, lunar, Mars, freight, rescue, communications, and private infrastructure markets. Federal procurement remained decisive for combat systems and protected command equipment, but it no longer set the pace for every useful spacecraft category.</p><p>The sector's power lay in the combination of competing design houses and a common transport economy. A prime contractor could sell a vehicle to several operators; a carrier could replace one manufacturer's equipment with another's; and the Aerospace Force could purchase or adapt a capability first financed for commerce. The complete register of manufacturers, surviving corporate names, consolidation, program assignments, facilities, and employment remains open.</p>"}
    ],
    related:[...americanOrbitalDoctrineRelated,{href:"commercial-spacecraft-procurement-united-states.html",kicker:"Market transition",label:"Commercial Spacecraft Procurement in the United States"},{href:"american-space-propulsion-industry.html",kicker:"Engine and power sector",label:"American Space-Propulsion Industry"},{href:"frontier-orbital-lines.html",kicker:"Principal operator",label:"Frontier Orbital Lines"},{href:"liberty-yard.html",kicker:"Orbital assembly site",label:"Liberty Yard"}],
    facts:[["Principal industrial form","Competing private design houses and suppliers"],["Early principal customer","United States Aerospace Force"],["Commercial transition","1985 onward"],["Procurement inversion","After 1990"],["Principal manufacturing base","Terrestrial factories"],["Orbital role","Assembly · integration · repair · large structures"],["Operator/manufacturer distinction","Retained"],["Exact prime contractors","Open"],["Article horizon","5 January 2012"]]
  }),

  "commercial-spacecraft-procurement-united-states": americanOrbitalDoctrineArticle({
    title:"Commercial spacecraft procurement in the United States",
    eyebrow:"United States · company requirements and manufacturer projects after 1985",
    infoboxKicker:"American spacecraft-acquisition system",
    lead:"Commercial spacecraft procurement in the United States is the system through which private operators and established aerospace manufacturers increasingly initiated spacecraft independently of federal requirements after about 1985. Corporate requirements and internal manufacturer designations outgrew the federal register after 1990, while government procurement continued for military and prestige programs.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='a'>[a]</sup>",
    canon:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-p3c7g writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T10:40:00-06:00 -->Company-funded spacecraft, continuing participation by established aviation manufacturers, the growing importance of internal designations after about 1985, post-1990 corporate predominance, federal austerity, survival of later O-series vehicles, and continued military procurement are directly established.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='a'>[a]</sup> The Commercial Orbital Transportation Act title and date, exact government-control allocation, 1990–95 phase boundaries, detailed acquisition paths, finance and interface regime, and the O-101 through O-109 requirement register are incorporated reconstruction or open.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->",
    sections:[
      {id:"before",title:"Procurement before 1985",html:"<p>Early American orbital vehicles began with a government requirement. Federal laboratories reduced technical risk, private contractors competed, and the Aerospace Force accepted a vehicle into the O-series. Public discussion usually centered on the service designation rather than the manufacturer's internal proposal number.</p>"},
      {id:"act",title:"Working legal reconstruction",html:`<p>The named 1985 Commercial Orbital Transportation Act is an incorporated legal mechanism for licensed operators to acquire Aerospace Force-derived spacecraft and supporting equipment. Its range-control, traffic, docking, rescue, military-payload, and emergency-requisition provisions are extrapolated.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup></p><p>Direct canon establishes only that the first private carrier formed around 1985, obtained older government spaceplanes, and entered the corporate transport market.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='a'>[a]</sup> Its precise initial aircraft, parts, simulators, tooling, facilities, and anchor contracts remain reconstructed.</p>`},
      {id:"requirements",title:"Company requirements",html:"<p>A carrier could specify turnaround, payload interfaces, crew accommodation, operating cost, docking standards, or route endurance for its own business. A station operator might seek a construction tug; a communications company an automatic service craft; a mining concern a slow high-efficiency freighter.</p><p>Manufacturers could respond with proprietary projects before a federal office recognized the same market. The commercial customer paid for a transportation or industrial function rather than a military mission category. Its evaluation emphasized lifetime operating cost, fleet commonality, maintenance access, insurance, financing, and the ability to earn revenue across several routes.</p><p>Operators were not necessarily manufacturers. Frontier could define a route and cabin standard, maintain and modify its fleet, and sponsor a development without becoming the clean-sheet designer of every airframe, engine, or avionics set. That distinction allowed the same industrial houses to compete for federal, carrier, and independent corporate work.</p>"},
      {id:"paths",title:"Acquisition paths",html:"<div class='table-wrap'><table class='wiki-table'><thead><tr><th>Path</th><th>Typical sequence</th><th>Public identity</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Federal requirement</td><td>Mission definition → competition → test status → service acceptance</td><td>O, F, A, B, or X/Y-prefixed service designation</td></tr><tr><td>Carrier procurement</td><td>Route or fleet need → manufacturer proposals → carrier order</td><td>Manufacturer model and operator name</td></tr><tr><td>Company-funded project</td><td>Market forecast → internal development → demonstrator → customers</td><td>Internal project number or product name</td></tr><tr><td>Commercial derivative</td><td>Federal or private design → altered payload, crew, or operating standard</td><td>Company mark, carrier fleet name, or later federal designation</td></tr><tr><td>Joint rescue</td><td>Threatened federal program → shared finance → small public fleet plus private derivative</td><td>Parallel service and commercial identities</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},
      {id:"numbers",title:"Manufacturer designations",html:`<p>After roughly 1985, an internal project number became a first-class identity rather than an archival footnote. Different firms retained different numbering and naming practices; the United States did not impose one common commercial register.</p><p>A vehicle acquired later by the Aerospace Force could receive an O, F, A, or B designation. A wholly commercial craft could spend its complete service life under the manufacturer's designation and the carrier's fleet name.${americanOrbitalDoctrineCanon}</p>`},
      {id:"crisis",title:"Procurement inversion, 1990–1995",html:`<p>The Open Door Depression sharply reduced federal purchasing. Several late O-Hundred requirements approached cancellation. The Aerospace Force preserved its unique deep-space mission through smaller production blocks, delayed schedules, shared funding, contractor ownership, and commercial derivatives. The institutional fact that these were an armed service's programs, rather than the projects of a separable civilian space bureau, helped prevent wholesale abandonment.</p><p>Meanwhile carriers and industrial customers continued buying transport and infrastructure. By the middle of the decade, the federal government increasingly selected from capabilities already being developed for a private market. Corporate outlays and the variety of private requirements began to exceed those generated by federal procurement, even though government research, ranges, licensing, and protected missions remained indispensable.</p>`},
      {id:"periods",title:"Phases of the commercial transition",html:"<div class='table-wrap'><table class='wiki-table'><thead><tr><th>Period</th><th>Procurement balance</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>1985–1989</td><td>Government requirements still define the prestige frontier; licensed firms acquire and adapt public designs.</td></tr><tr><td>1990–1995</td><td>Federal austerity forces triage while carriers and industrial customers sustain orders, shared programs, and derivatives.</td></tr><tr><td>Late 1990s</td><td>Commercial transport and infrastructure provide the larger field of routine production and vehicle variety.</td></tr><tr><td>2000s–2012</td><td>Corporations carry most routine American access to space; the Aerospace Force concentrates its original procurement on military and strategic spacecraft.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>The boundaries are descriptive rather than statutory. Public and private purchasing overlapped throughout the transition.</p>"},
      {id:"risk",title:"Finance, risk, and standards",html:"<p>Commercial procurement shifted risk without removing it. An operator could place a launch order before a vehicle existed, a manufacturer could fund a demonstrator against several expected customers, and a federal office could pay for a specialized derivative rather than the original design. Insurers, lenders, station owners, and traffic authorities consequently influenced configuration through certification and interoperability requirements even when they did not buy the craft.</p><p>A successful interface could matter as much as a successful airframe. Docking fittings, cargo modules, propellant couplings, communications, software, ground support, and crew qualifications tied vehicles into an operating network. A technically sound project could fail if it imposed a separate infrastructure burden; a conservative derivative could prosper by fitting equipment already used by several carriers.</p><p>Exact financing instruments, subsidy shares, order quantities, and proprietary standards are not fixed in the accepted record.</p>"},
      {id:"consequences",title:"Effect on the later O-series",html:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-p4d8h writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T10:40:00-06:00 --><p>The author directly establishes that later O-series utility and experimental requirements were overtaken by corporate procurement and came close to cancellation under post-1990 austerity, while the Aerospace Force kept them alive as its distinctive programs.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='a'>[a]</sup></p><p>The exact O-101 through O-109 number-and-role register, and the claim that particular commercial tugs, depots, or construction services dated each specification before entry, are incorporated extrapolation.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup></p><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->"},
      {id:"oversight",title:"Oversight and strategic control",html:"<p>Commercial ownership did not remove public authority over traffic, safety, strategic payloads, rescue, communications coordination, and emergency service. National-carrier charters and procurement clauses allowed the government to preserve capacity it no longer operated directly.</p>"}
    ],
    related:[...americanOrbitalDoctrineRelated,{href:"american-spacecraft-manufacturing-industry.html",kicker:"Supplier network",label:"American Spacecraft-Manufacturing Industry"},{href:"commercial-orbital-transportation-act.html",kicker:"Enabling law",label:"Commercial Orbital Transportation Act"},{href:"frontier-orbital-lines.html",kicker:"Principal carrier",label:"Frontier Orbital Lines"},{href:"national-orbital-carrier.html",kicker:"Public-service charter",label:"National Orbital Carrier"},{href:"open-door-depression.html",kicker:"Procurement crisis",label:"Open Door Depression"}],
    facts:[["Working opening statute","Commercial Orbital Transportation Act · 1985 <sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup>"],["Initial model","Government requirement followed by contractor competition"],["Later model","Customer or manufacturer initiative <sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='a'>[a]</sup>"],["Procurement inversion","After 1990 <sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='a'>[a]</sup>"],["Public identity after 1985","Manufacturer names and internal project numbers <sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='a'>[a]</sup>"],["Federal designation","Assigned when accepted for service"],["Later O-series survival","Directly established; exact remedies extrapolated"],["Exact contracts and prices","Open"],["Article horizon","5 January 2012"]]
  }),

  "american-orbital-combat-aircraft": americanOrbitalDoctrineArticle({
    title:"Orbital combat aircraft of the United States",
    eyebrow:"United States Aerospace Force · orbit-only F, A, and B missions",
    infoboxKicker:"American military spacecraft category",
    lead:"Orbital combat aircraft of the United States are purpose-built Aerospace Force fighters, attack craft, and strategic bombers intended to operate permanently outside an atmosphere. Their development followed the transfer of routine transport and logistics to commercial carriers around the turn of the century. The service returned to the ordinary F, A, and B mission letters because it classified the vehicles by military role rather than by atmospheric capability. Crews remained pilots, formations remained squadrons, and operations remained sorties.",
    canon:"The post-2000 military refocus, return to F/A/B mission designations, orbit-only operation, aircraft terminology, carrier-support concept, and broad combat roles are established. The first accepted type numbers, contractors, names, dimensions, propulsion, weapons, unit structure, inventory, bases, and readiness dates remain open.",
    sections:[
      {id:"predecessors",title:"Predecessors",html:`<p><a href='project-zenith.html'>O-7A Zenith</a> combined inspection, interception, electronic warfare, strategic maneuver, and limited conventional reentry attack within an atmospheric spaceplane. The O-105 requirement later reserved a cislunar patrol and inspection craft that no longer had to return to a runway.</p><p>These programs supplied sensors, rendezvous practice, secure communications, weapons-release rules, maneuver planning, and crew experience. They did not settle the configuration of the dedicated 2000s combat generation.</p>`},
      {id:"refocus",title:"Military refocus after 2000",html:"<p>Private carriers, tugs, depots, and contractors assumed much of the scheduled movement, satellite service, propellant delivery, and station construction formerly performed by military units. The Aerospace Force concentrated new procurement on tasks for which no ordinary commercial customer existed: combat, protected surveillance, secured command, defended bases, and strategic maneuver.</p><p>This was a change in emphasis rather than an end to military transport. The service continued to control strategic traffic and operate specialized utility craft, but no longer needed each new spacecraft to justify itself as a general-purpose carrier. A combat design could be smaller, shorter-ranged, more highly accelerated, or more heavily equipped because tankers, depots, and base facilities existed to support it.</p>"},
      {id:"designation",title:"Mission designations",html:`<p>The Joint Aircraft Designation Act defines F by the fighter mission, A by attack, and B by strategic bombardment. The Aerospace Force therefore reused those sequences for spacecraft unable to enter an atmosphere. O remained the operational-vehicle sequence for orbital utility, transport, patrol, and exploration requirements.</p><div class='table-wrap'><table class='wiki-table'><thead><tr><th>Letter</th><th>Orbital mission</th><th>Typical employment</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>F</td><td>Interception and protection</td><td>Rapid response, escort, sensor contact, hostile-vehicle interception</td></tr><tr><td>A</td><td>Attack</td><td>Close orbital attack, station or vehicle mission kill, support of defended operations</td></tr><tr><td>B</td><td>Strategic strike</td><td>Long-range weapons carriage, protected release, strategic maneuver</td></tr><tr><td>O</td><td>Orbital utility</td><td>Transport, tanker, rescue, patrol, construction, survey, and endurance requirements</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>No accepted F-, A-, or B-series orbital type number is fixed by the current reference.${americanOrbitalDoctrineCanon}</p>`},
      {id:"organization",title:"Crews and organization",html:"<p>The service retained aviation language. Personnel qualified as pilots and mission specialists. Aircraft belonged to flights and squadrons; maintenance units measured serviceability and sortie generation. Carrier-borne units added embarked maintenance, magazines, fuel, rescue, and handling crews.</p><p>The terminology expressed institutional identity. Aerospace officers treated orbit-only craft as the continuation of American military aviation even when Navy and Army observers described the same machines as small warships. Mission planning therefore borrowed the language of interception, escort, strike packages, alert aircraft, sorties, and combat air patrol rather than adopting a wholly naval register.</p><p>The exact number of crew aboard each class, degree of automation, squadron strength, and command relationship to Orbital Security Command remain open.</p>"},
      {id:"architecture",title:"Vehicle architecture",html:"<p>Combat craft retained a fixed forward axis, dorsal flight deck, ventral protection, aft propulsion, and bilateral systems arrangement. The convention was cultural and operational rather than aerodynamic: crews, docks, maintenance stands, sensor sectors, and approach procedures all benefited from a stable definition of forward, dorsal, ventral, port, and starboard.</p><p>Carrier-supported vehicles could sacrifice independent endurance, large workshops, and some consumables for sensors, maneuver propellant, weapons, and acceleration. Independent patrol designs required greater life support and repair capacity. Exact divisions between carrier aircraft, base aircraft, automatic interceptors, and crewed craft remain open.</p>"},
      {id:"weapons",title:"Weapons and engagement",html:`<p>Guided intercept vehicles and missiles supplied maneuver after release and engagement reach. Guns served close defense, terminal interception, sensor attack, missile defense, and short-range mission kills. Full-velocity kinetic strikes remained capable of defeating practical local armor.</p><p>The three mission letters did not imply atmospheric dogfighting. An interceptor sought a useful future geometry, forced identification, protected a station or convoy, and threatened a vehicle's sensors, propulsion, or mission equipment. Attack aircraft concentrated on defended local targets and support of orbital operations. Bombers carried strategic weapons across longer distances or held them in protected maneuvering positions.</p><p>Debris rules and the <a href='stockholm-orbital-safety-convention.html'>Stockholm orbital-safety system</a> discouraged indiscriminate fire through heavily used orbital lanes. A successful hit could require stabilization, salvage, evacuation, or controlled disposal rather than automatic deorbit.</p>`},
      {id:"support",title:"Basing and support",html:`<p>Combat squadrons could operate from defended stations, depots, yards, or a proposed <a href='american-orbital-carrier-requirement.html'>orbital carrier</a>. Fixed bases offered deeper stores and repair facilities; a carrier could move aircraft, crews, weapons, and sensors toward a useful orbital plane or cislunar route.</p><p>Commercial tankers, yards, communications, and traffic services provided part of the industrial substrate while protected command, weapons custody, rules of engagement, and military communications remained governmental. Mobilization plans could draw upon <a href='merchant-aerospace-united-states.html'>merchant aerospace</a>, but commercial support did not turn licensed carriers into ordinary combat squadrons.</p>`},
      {id:"availability",title:"Operational availability and attrition",html:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-m-20260816-k7d4p writer=m kind=analysis created=2026-08-16T14:03:00-06:00 --><p>A squadron establishment did not equal an immediately usable combat force. Employment required a serviceable aircraft in useful orbital geometry, a qualified crew, maneuver propellant, weapons, functioning sensors and secure communications, and access to tracking, rescue, tanker, magazine, and maintenance support. A technically serviceable craft could therefore remain irrelevant to a particular interception because its orbit, crew state, or supporting movement imposed more delay than the warning allowed. Readiness was a property of the assembled package and its time to contact, not of the hull alone.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup></p><p>Combat loss also had stages. Destruction of a vehicle was different from loss of its crew, and both differed from a mission kill that left the craft stable enough for evacuation, towing, or repair. Debris-control rules and <a href='american-spacecraft-protection.html'>damage-control practice</a> encouraged stabilization and recovery where circumstances permitted. A damaged aircraft could nevertheless subtract several assets from the next operation by occupying a tug, dock, repair team, escort, and traffic window. The operational cost of a hit therefore depended upon the time and resources needed to return the whole support system to readiness.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup></p><p>Replacement extended beyond manufacturing another airframe. Pilots and mission specialists required training; maintainers needed experience with the accepted type; protected electronics, weapons, propellant, spares, and serialized assemblies had to reach the correct base or carrier and pass military control. Commercial transport could move people, parts, and consumables, but could not by itself replace weapons custody, combat command, or service-specific integration. In a prolonged conflict the Aerospace Force would have to convert national lift and industrial capacity into sustained squadron output while repairing damaged craft and replacing scarce personnel.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup></p><p>The accepted record does not establish an inventory, alert rate, repair time, replacement pipeline, or combat-loss history for the F-, A-, and B-series generation. Comparisons with German, Japanese, or other orbital forces must therefore distinguish authorized establishment, aircraft actually in orbit, aircraft on alert, and aircraft able to reach the disputed geometry within the decision period.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup></p><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->"},
      {id:"institutional",title:"Institutional meaning",html:"<p>The new aircraft marked the Aerospace Force's transition from an experimental and utility-heavy branch into a claimant on the principal combat budget. Its officers argued that orbital combat was the mature form of the aviation mission from which the service had begun. The Navy instead saw armed, carrier-based spacecraft and a mobile orbital base as naval functions translated into another environment.</p><p>This difference shaped appropriations as much as terminology. A production fleet of F-, A-, and B-designated spacecraft would compete directly with Navy and Army modernization rather than remaining a small scientific or prestige account.</p>"},
      {id:"status",title:"Status in 2012",html:"<p>Purpose-built orbital F, A, and B categories existed as a procurement direction by 5 January 2012. The accepted aircraft register, manufacturers, operational units, readiness dates, weapons loads, and whether any type had reached routine squadron service remain unregistered.</p>"}
    ],
    related:[...americanOrbitalDoctrineRelated,{href:"project-zenith.html",kicker:"Armed spaceplane predecessor",label:"Project Zenith"},{href:"american-orbital-carrier-requirement.html",kicker:"Mobile base requirement",label:"American Orbital-Carrier Requirement"},{href:"orbital-weapons.html",kicker:"Weapons and legal context",label:"Orbital Weapons"},{href:"joint-aircraft-designation-act.html",kicker:"Designation framework",label:"Joint Aircraft Designation Act"}],
    facts:[["Service","United States Aerospace Force"],["Development period","2000s onward"],["Operating environment","Orbit and cislunar space"],["Atmospheric certification","Not required"],["Mission letters","F · A · B"],["Utility sequence","O retained"],["Unit terminology","Flights · squadrons · sorties"],["Accepted type numbers","Open"],["Article horizon","5 January 2012"]]
  }),

  "american-spacecraft-protection": americanOrbitalDoctrineArticle({
    title:"American spacecraft protection and damage control",
    category:"Military technology",
    eyebrow:"United States · spacecraft survivability · 1960s–2012",
    infoboxKicker:"American spacecraft survivability practice",
    infoboxTitle:"Spacecraft protection and damage control",
    lead:`American spacecraft protection and damage control is the American practice of combining passive shielding, local armor, separated pressure volumes, redundant services, active defense, repair, and trained crew response to preserve a vehicle after debris impact, mechanical failure, or hostile damage. The author establishes that protective mass remains useful in space and that a large military carrier should be armored in a spacecraft-appropriate sense.${americanOrbitalDoctrineCanon} The detailed architecture is incorporated engineering reconstruction rather than a fixed armor specification.${americanOrbitalDoctrineExtrapolation}`,
    canon:`The usefulness of protective mass, the need for a substantially protected American orbital carrier, the inability of armor to make a ship invulnerable to high-energy guided impact, and the wider orbital-debris problem are established.${americanOrbitalDoctrineCanon} Sacrificial bumpers, stuffed standoff layers, local heavy shielding, water and stores as useful protective mass, pressure compartmentation, duplicated buses, distributed control, isolation valves, reserve navigation, repair teams, emergency shelters, and the proposed threat-response hierarchy are incorporated technical development.${americanOrbitalDoctrineExtrapolation} No standard armor thickness, protection rating, fleet-wide manual, combat-loss history, approved carrier design, or named protection system is fixed by 5 January 2012.`,
    sections:[
      {id:"scope",title:"Scope and purpose",html:`<p>Protection begins from the assumption that avoiding every strike is impossible. Natural micrometeoroids, human-made debris, failed dockings, ruptured machinery, fragments, and weapons can all penetrate exposed structure or disable equipment. The design objective is therefore not an unbroken outer shell, but survival of the people and functions needed to control, stabilize, repair, evacuate, or safely dispose of the vehicle.${americanOrbitalDoctrineExtrapolation}</p><p>The practice spans hull design, equipment placement, pressure management, electrical distribution, software, stores, rescue interfaces, maintenance organization, and crew drills. It is most developed for military ships and long-duration craft, but many of its methods begin in ordinary civil spacecraft safety.${americanOrbitalDoctrineExtrapolation}</p>`},
      {id:"threats",title:"Threat environment",html:`<p>American designers distinguish threats by energy, warning, direction, and likely consequence. A small fast particle may threaten one pressure wall; a nearby explosion may distribute fragments across radiators and sensors; a maneuvering interceptor may select a reactor, magazine, command space, or propulsion system; a full-velocity guided impact can exceed any practical local protection.${americanOrbitalDoctrineExtrapolation}</p><div class='table-wrap'><table class='wiki-table'><thead><tr><th>Threat</th><th>Primary response</th><th>Principal limit</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Micrometeoroids and small debris</td><td>Standoff bumpers, layered shielding, detection, repair</td><td>Repeated impacts and exposed equipment</td></tr><tr><td>Large tracked debris</td><td>Warning, maneuver, interception, sheltering</td><td>Late detection or insufficient maneuver time</td></tr><tr><td>Fragments and near misses</td><td>Distributed shielding, isolation, redundancy</td><td>Simultaneous damage across a wide area</td></tr><tr><td>Close weapon or small kinetic strike</td><td>Active defense, local armor, compartmentation</td><td>Critical-system hit or multiple penetrations</td></tr><tr><td>High-energy guided impact</td><td>Detection, deception, interception, maneuver</td><td>No plausible passive shell guarantees survival</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>The hierarchy is an incorporated description of design practice, not a settled national protection-rating table.${americanOrbitalDoctrineExtrapolation}</p>`},
      {id:"shielding",title:"Layered external shielding",html:`<p>The basic passive barrier uses a sacrificial outer bumper separated from the pressure structure. An incoming particle strikes the first layer, breaks up, spreads, or vaporizes, and reaches deeper layers as a less concentrated cloud. Intermediate fabrics, ceramics, foams, and structural panels can absorb or disperse additional energy before the remains reach the pressure wall.${americanOrbitalDoctrineExtrapolation}</p><p>Standoff distance is part of the shield. A thin outer skin placed directly on the pressure hull does less useful work than a layered system with room for the debris cloud to spread. Different regions therefore receive different spacing and materials according to expected approach directions, access requirements, and the equipment mounted outside.${americanOrbitalDoctrineExtrapolation}</p>`},
      {id:"local-armor",title:"Local armor and useful mass",html:`<p>American studies reject equal protection around the entire vehicle. Heavy local shielding is concentrated around command spaces, reactors, magazines, propellant trunks, power conversion, critical computers, pressure refuges, and equipment whose destruction would prevent safe recovery. Less consequential structure may be allowed to fail while the ship remains recoverable.${americanOrbitalDoctrineExtrapolation}</p><p>Water, food, stores, machinery, and propellant can be arranged so that mass already required by the mission also lies between a likely threat direction and occupied or critical compartments. This useful mass supplements the designed shield while avoiding some of the added weight imposed by dedicated armor.${americanOrbitalDoctrineExtrapolation}</p>`},
      {id:"compartments",title:"Pressure compartmentation",html:`<p>A long-duration or military spacecraft is divided into pressure sections that can be isolated after penetration. Automatic and manual valves close atmosphere, coolant, propellant, and service connections across a damaged boundary. Crew members in an affected section move to a refuge or neighboring volume before pressure becomes unrecoverable.${americanOrbitalDoctrineExtrapolation}</p><p>Compartmentation imposes doors, seals, duplicated controls, passage restrictions, inspection burdens, and additional structural mass. Its value appears after a hit: loss of one workshop, passage, berth, or equipment room need not empty the complete inhabited hull.${americanOrbitalDoctrineExtrapolation}</p>`},
      {id:"atmosphere",title:"Atmosphere, fire, and contamination",html:`<p>Damage control treats pressure loss, fire, smoke, toxic leakage, and cooling failure as related hazards. A penetrated compartment may be isolated before repair; a fire may be suppressed by local agents, atmosphere reduction, or complete evacuation; contaminated air and coolant must be prevented from circulating through surviving spaces.${americanOrbitalDoctrineExtrapolation}</p><p>Portable breathing equipment, pressure garments, patching material, fire monitors, gas sensors, and protected refuge capacity give crews time to respond. The exact equipment differs between a small combat aircraft, a commercial transport, a station, and a capital-spacecraft study.${americanOrbitalDoctrineExtrapolation}</p>`},
      {id:"services",title:"Power, data, and control redundancy",html:`<p>Separated electrical and data buses prevent one penetration from cutting every route between generation, storage, control, and load. Cross-ties permit surviving sections to be reconfigured, while breakers and isolation switches prevent a short or damaged cable from collapsing the rest of the system.${americanOrbitalDoctrineExtrapolation}</p><p>Attitude control is distributed among several clusters rather than concentrated at one point. Reserve navigation and control computers retain a reduced ability to determine attitude, communicate, and command thrusters after the principal flight deck or sensor chain is lost. Protected cable trunks and physically separated command paths matter as much as duplicate software.${americanOrbitalDoctrineExtrapolation}</p>`},
      {id:"critical",title:"Propulsion, reactors, and magazines",html:`<p>Propellant and weapons create dangers beyond penetration. Lines and tanks require shutoff points, drainage or venting routes, leak detection, and separation from inhabited spaces. Magazines use local protection, controlled access, and physical distance from command and reactor compartments where the design permits it.${americanOrbitalDoctrineExtrapolation}</p><p>Reactor shielding must protect crews during normal operation as well as after damage. Shutdown, decay-heat removal, reserve cooling, and electrical isolation remain necessary even if the main propulsion or power mission has ended. A disabled reactor or propulsion unit may be abandoned locally while the rest of the vehicle provides refuge and communications.${americanOrbitalDoctrineExtrapolation}</p>`},
      {id:"external",title:"External systems and graceful degradation",html:`<p>Radiators, antennas, optical instruments, docking fixtures, maneuvering thrusters, and sensor arrays cannot all be buried behind armor. Designers instead separate them into sectors, provide alternate units, protect retraction or isolation mechanisms, and preserve reduced modes of operation after part of the exterior is lost.${americanOrbitalDoctrineExtrapolation}</p><p>A ship may survive pressure damage yet become mission-incapable through loss of heat rejection, navigation, communications, or attitude control. Protection doctrine therefore measures survival in stages: preservation of life, stabilization, withdrawal or rescue, restoration of movement, and only then continuation of the assigned mission.${americanOrbitalDoctrineExtrapolation}</p>`},
      {id:"active",title:"Active defense and avoidance",html:`<p>Passive protection is the final layer rather than the first. Tracking systems and the <a href='orbital-debris-warning-system.html'>orbital debris warning network</a> provide time to maneuver around large objects. Military sensors, decoys, electronic measures, guided interceptors, and close-defense weapons attempt to prevent a hostile vehicle or projectile from reaching the hull.${americanOrbitalDoctrineCanon}</p><p>Guns are most useful for terminal interception, fragments, exposed sensors, and short-range mission kills. Guided weapons provide greater reach and can maneuver after release. Neither removes the need for shielding: warning can arrive late, an interceptor can leak through, and even a successful defense can produce dangerous fragments.${americanOrbitalDoctrineExtrapolation}</p>`},
      {id:"repair",title:"Repair and recovery organization",html:`<p>Damage-control organization links engineering design to crew action. Watch personnel identify the damaged zone, isolate services, account for crew, stabilize attitude and power, fight fire, patch pressure boundaries, and decide whether a compartment can be re-entered. Remote cameras and sensors reduce exposure but do not eliminate manual work.${americanOrbitalDoctrineExtrapolation}</p><p>Repair lockers, portable seals, cable bypasses, hose connections, structural braces, medical supplies, and maneuvering equipment are distributed so that one hit does not remove every tool. A large ship can support specialized teams and workshops; a small craft depends more heavily on automatic isolation, simple replaceable units, and external rescue.${americanOrbitalDoctrineExtrapolation}</p>`},
      {id:"carrier",title:"Orbital-carrier application",html:`<p>The proposed <a href='american-orbital-carrier-requirement.html'>American orbital carrier</a> makes damage control a capital-spacecraft requirement. Its hangar, magazines, propellant storage, workshops, reactors, habitation, command spaces, and embarked aircraft create many different hazards within one mobile base. Protection must prevent an accident or strike in one function from cascading through the rest.${americanOrbitalDoctrineCanon}</p><p>The carrier combines layered exterior barriers, local protection, separated pressure zones, duplicated services, reserve control, emergency shelters, repair capacity, and an active defensive screen. Its scale permits more redundancy than a fighter or tug, but also creates more exposed area and a larger mass penalty. No approved arrangement, shield depth, compartment plan, or commissioned class is fixed.${americanOrbitalDoctrineExtrapolation}</p>`},
      {id:"civil",title:"Civil and merchant application",html:`<p><a href='merchant-aerospace-united-states.html'>Merchant spacecraft</a> require protection against debris, collision, fire, pressure loss, and machinery failure even when they carry no weapons. Passenger capacity, route duration, rescue distance, insurance, maintenance intervals, and docking frequency determine how much redundancy and refuge space an operator can justify.${americanOrbitalDoctrineExtrapolation}</p><p>Military-compatible merchant auxiliaries may add protected communications, reserve power, conversion fittings, or strengthened local spaces. These provisions do not make every commercial hull a warship. They make licensed capacity easier to preserve and employ during rescue, emergency requisition, or national service.${americanOrbitalDoctrineExtrapolation}</p>`},
      {id:"law",title:"Debris control and orbital law",html:`<p>Protection practice operates inside a legal order intended to prevent one damaged craft from endangering many others. The <a href='stockholm-orbital-safety-convention.html'>Stockholm Orbital Safety Convention</a>, notification rules, rescue duties, tracking networks, and the broader body of <a href='orbital-law.html'>orbital law</a> require reporting, stabilization, assistance, and controlled disposal where practicable.${americanOrbitalDoctrineCanon}</p><p>The principle of <a href='orbital-debris-neutral-harm.html'>debris-neutral harm</a> also shapes weapons and self-defense. A commander may be able to disable a target without being free to fill a common route with fragments. Damage-control capacity supports compliance by making salvage, towing, evacuation, and controlled deorbit more plausible after an engagement.${americanOrbitalDoctrineExtrapolation}</p>`},
      {id:"mass",title:"Mass, cost, and design limits",html:`<p>Every bumper, shield layer, duplicated bus, pressure door, refuge, spare, and repair team competes with payload, propellant, weapons, radiators, accommodation, or operating economy. Protection is therefore allocated by consequence and probability rather than distributed uniformly.${americanOrbitalDoctrineExtrapolation}</p><p>The central design question is not whether armor has mass, but which additional mass most improves survival. Sometimes the answer is a shield; elsewhere it is a second control path, a farther separation between tanks, more maneuver propellant, a better warning sensor, or the ability to isolate and abandon one section. No amount of mass abolishes orbital weapon physics.${americanOrbitalDoctrineCanon}</p>`},
      {id:"status",title:"Position by 2012",html:`<p>By 5 January 2012 layered debris protection, compartmentation, redundancy, emergency isolation, repair, and rescue interfaces belong to mature American spacecraft practice. The proposed carrier extends them into a deliberately armored military capital craft, but its final specification and service history remain open.${americanOrbitalDoctrineCanon}</p><p>The accepted record does not establish a common fleet-wide protection scale, named doctrine manual, armor material standard, combat damage survey, or purpose-built damage-control service. Those details should remain open until additional authorial canon assigns them.${americanOrbitalDoctrineExtrapolation}</p>`}
    ],
    related:[...americanOrbitalDoctrineRelated,{href:"american-orbital-carrier-requirement.html",kicker:"Capital-spacecraft application",label:"American Orbital-Carrier Requirement"},{href:"american-orbital-combat-aircraft.html",kicker:"Smaller military craft",label:"Orbital Combat Aircraft of the United States"},{href:"orbital-weapons.html",kicker:"Threat environment",label:"Orbital Weapons"},{href:"orbital-debris-warning-system.html",kicker:"Collision warning",label:"Orbital Debris Warning System"},{href:"stockholm-orbital-safety-convention.html",kicker:"Safety regime",label:"Stockholm Orbital Safety Convention"},{href:"orbital-law.html",kicker:"Legal setting",label:"Orbital Law"},{href:"merchant-aerospace-united-states.html",kicker:"Civil and auxiliary application",label:"Merchant Aerospace in the United States"},{href:"american-spacecraft-manufacturing-industry.html",kicker:"Industrial implementation",label:"American Spacecraft-Manufacturing Industry"}],
    facts:[["National tradition","United States"],["Principal model","Layered shielding · local armor · compartmentation"],["First defense","Detection · avoidance · interception"],["Pressure survival","Isolated sections and emergency refuge"],["Service survival","Separated power, data, control, and cooling"],["Critical-space protection","Command · reactors · magazines · propellant systems"],["Primary design limit","Mass and system complexity"],["Absolute protection","Not achievable"],["Capital application","American orbital-carrier requirement"],["Common protection rating","Open"],["Article horizon","5 January 2012"]],
    categories:["American spacecraft protection and damage control","American spaceflight","United States","Military technology","Spacecraft design","Orbital warfare"]
  }),

  "american-orbital-carrier-requirement": americanOrbitalDoctrineArticle({
    title:"American orbital-carrier requirement",
    eyebrow:"United States Aerospace Force · capital-spacecraft studies",
    infoboxKicker:"American military spacecraft requirement",
    lead:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-k7m3t writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T09:33:00-06:00 -->The author directly establishes a 2000s Aerospace Force desire for a true orbital carrier: a mobile base for specialized fighters, attackers, and bombers, sufficiently large and costly to provoke serious interservice budget conflict.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='a'>[a]</sup> Its combined airfield, tanker, magazine, repair, sensor, command, rescue, and crew functions are incorporated requirement development, and no commissioned class or construction status is fixed by 5 January 2012.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->",
    canon:"The 2000s orbital-carrier requirement, mobile support for purpose-built combat aircraft, very large scale, some protective mass, and interservice budget conflict are direct authorial direction. The bilateral configuration, longitudinal hangar, paired handling bays, layered protection, compartmentalized damage control, guided offensive weapons, close-defense guns, and complete functional list are incorporated design development. Authorization, builder, ship name, number ordered, dimensions, propulsion, embarked group, construction site, launch date, and commission remain open.",
    sections:[
      {id:"origin",title:"Origin",html:`<p>The Aerospace Force's first generations carried most of their own support because stations, tankers, yards, and rescue systems were sparse. The <a href='deep-space-century.html'>O-Hundred</a> and commercial generations created permanent orbital logistics. Purpose-built combat aircraft could then depend upon a larger mobile base.</p><p>The carrier requirement emerged as the service refocused on military tasks after routine transportation moved to licensed operators.</p>`},
      {id:"purpose",title:"Operational purpose",html:"<p>Orbital engagements depend upon position, plane, timing, available velocity change, sensor coverage, and support. A carrier moves a group of smaller craft and their stores toward a useful operating geometry before a crisis. It reduces the fuel, life support, maintenance equipment, and spare parts each combat aircraft must carry.</p><p>The carrier is therefore less a launch platform in the maritime sense than a mobile concentration of endurance and support. It changes where a squadron can wait, refuel, repair, rearm, and receive command information. Its value is greatest before an engagement, when a change of plane or transfer opportunity can determine which force arrives with useful propellant remaining.</p><p>The concept does not require the carrier to maneuver into every engagement. Embarked craft and guided weapons extend its sensors and magazines away from the principal hull.</p>"},
      {id:"functions",title:"Required functions",html:"<div class='table-wrap'><table class='wiki-table'><thead><tr><th>Function</th><th>Required capacity</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Flight operations</td><td>Paired handling bays, launch and recovery control, servicing positions</td></tr><tr><td>Propellant</td><td>Storage, transfer, conditioning, isolation, and replenishment</td></tr><tr><td>Maintenance</td><td>Workshops, spares, test equipment, and damaged-aircraft handling</td></tr><tr><td>Weapons</td><td>Protected magazines, checkout, custody, and loading spaces</td></tr><tr><td>Sensors and command</td><td>Long-range surveillance, communications, computing, battle management</td></tr><tr><td>Crew support</td><td>Habitation, medical care, shelters, stores, and endurance</td></tr><tr><td>Rescue</td><td>Recovery craft, pressure refuge, casualty treatment, and towing support</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},
      {id:"configuration",title:"Configuration",html:`<p>American studies use a longitudinal keel, aft propulsion cluster, forward-dorsal command citadel, principal axial hangar, and port-and-starboard aircraft handling bays. Cargo, propellant, radiators, sensors, and servicing structures are arranged bilaterally around the changing center of mass.</p><p>The fixed orientation reflects the wider <a href='american-spacecraft-design.html'>American spacecraft-design tradition</a>. Even an orbit-only capital craft is laid out as though it has a recognizable top, bottom, nose, and tail. That decision simplifies repeated docking, embarked-aircraft handling, crew circulation, damage-control plans, and visual identification while making no claim that the carrier can fly in an atmosphere.</p><p>The result resembles an extremely broad aircraft or carrier seen from above, although no atmospheric flight is possible. Exact geometry remains part of the unsettled program competition.</p>`},
      {id:"protection",title:"Protection and damage control",html:"<p><a href='american-spacecraft-protection.html'>Protection and damage control</a> begins with a sacrificial outer bumper and standoff debris and fragment layers. Stuffed layers, pressure structure, local heavy shielding, water, stores, and machinery protect command spaces, reactors, magazines, propellant trunks, power conversion, and other critical systems. The object is not to surround the whole hull with equal thickness, but to place useful mass and dedicated protection where penetration would be catastrophic.</p><p>Independent pressure sections, isolation valves, separated power and data buses, distributed attitude control, reserve navigation, local firefighting, repair equipment, and emergency shelters allow the ship to continue operating after penetrations. Armor improves survival against debris, fragments, near misses, and smaller weapons; it cannot stop every guided high-velocity impact.</p><p>Mass remains the central objection. Every shield layer competes with propellant, weapons, aircraft, radiators, and stores. The requirement consequently treats survivability as a combination of distance, interception, redundancy, compartmentation, repair, and local armor rather than a single armored shell.</p>"},
      {id:"armament",title:"Armament",html:`<p>Guided interceptors and missiles supply the carrier's principal reach. Embarked aircraft move sensors and weapons away from the hull. Guns and short-range interceptors protect against missiles, debris, close vehicles, and exposed sensors.</p><p>Weapon employment remains constrained by orbital geometry and the risk of contaminating useful routes with uncontrolled debris. The exact armament, magazine depth, and division between ship and embarked weapons remain open.</p>`},
      {id:"air-group",title:"Embarked group",html:`<p>The requirement assumes specialized <a href='american-orbital-combat-aircraft.html'>fighters, attack craft, reconnaissance or electronic-warfare aircraft, tankers, tugs, and rescue vehicles</a>. A supported fighter can carry more maneuver propellant, sensors, or weapons because the carrier supplies overhaul, crew endurance, and large stores.</p><p>No class composition, aircraft types, squadron count, or sortie rate is fixed.</p>`},
      {id:"logistics",title:"Logistics and replenishment",html:`<p>The carrier would depend upon the wider American orbital economy rather than carry an entire campaign's supplies from Earth. Tankers, automatic freighters, yard craft, and <a href='merchant-aerospace-united-states.html'>merchant auxiliaries</a> could replenish propellant, consumables, spares, and ordinary stores. Military custody would remain necessary for weapons, protected components, and operational information.</p><p>This dependence cuts both ways. Commercial infrastructure makes a large mobile base feasible, but interruptions to depots, traffic control, or propellant production can limit it without penetrating the hull. The carrier requirement therefore includes repair and reserve capacity while leaving the exact balance between onboard endurance and external replenishment open.</p>`},
      {id:"politics",title:"Interservice politics",html:"<p>The proposal places the Aerospace Force in direct competition with the Navy's carrier and surface-combatant programs and with Army modernization. Its cost is high enough that congressional approval could displace other service procurement. The resulting <a href='interservice-rivalry-united-states.html'>interservice dispute</a> also concerns institutional status: a branch long associated with experimental vehicles and small production runs would become the sponsor of a capital program.</p><p>Aerospace advocates present the program as an assembly of existing yards, tankers, reactors, shielding, weapons, and combat craft. Navy critics describe it as a second navy funded outside naval appropriations, while Army critics question whether a distant orbital platform should outrank terrestrial modernization.</p><p>The proposal is politically plausible because each major subsystem has a prior industrial basis. That does not establish that Congress accepted the combined cost, selected a builder, or authorized construction.</p>"},
      {id:"status",title:"Status by 5 January 2012",html:"<p>The requirement is established as a serious study and procurement objective. The current record does not determine whether Congress authorized a class, a keel or central spine was laid, a hull was named, or a carrier was commissioned. <em>Bonhomme Richard</em> is not an assigned name.</p>"}
    ],
    related:[...americanOrbitalDoctrineRelated,{href:"american-orbital-combat-aircraft.html",kicker:"Embarked aircraft",label:"Orbital Combat Aircraft of the United States"},{href:"american-spacecraft-protection.html",kicker:"Shielding and damage control",label:"American Spacecraft Protection and Damage Control"},{href:"interservice-rivalry-united-states.html",kicker:"Budget and mission dispute",label:"Interservice Rivalry in the United States"},{href:"orbital-weapons.html",kicker:"Weapons and survivability",label:"Orbital Weapons"},{href:"merchant-aerospace-united-states.html",kicker:"Commercial logistics substrate",label:"Merchant Aerospace in the United States"},{href:"us-navy-postwar.html",kicker:"Budgetary rival",label:"United States Navy after the Second World War"}],
    facts:[["Sponsor","United States Aerospace Force"],["Development period","2000s"],["Type","Capital-spacecraft requirement"],["Primary function","Mobile base for orbital combat aircraft"],["Configuration","Longitudinal hangar · paired handling bays"],["Protection","Layered shielding · local armor · compartmentalization"],["Principal reach","Guided interceptors, missiles, and embarked aircraft"],["Commissioned class","Not established"],["Fixed ship name","None"],["Article horizon","5 January 2012"]]
  }),

  "merchant-aerospace-united-states": americanOrbitalDoctrineArticle({
    title:"Merchant aerospace in the United States",
    eyebrow:"United States · commercial carriers, reserve obligations, and armed auxiliaries",
    infoboxKicker:"American commercial-spaceflight system",
    lead:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-r6q2w writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T09:36:00-06:00 -->The author directly brings American corporate fleets close to private-navy capability while rejecting an independent corporate right to wage war; public rhetoric instead presents them as merchant, citizen-soldier, or auxiliary capacity available to the nation.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='a'>[a]</sup> Licensing, public-service obligations, requisition, reserve commissions, conversion clauses, military-compatible systems, and limited defensive weapons are incorporated legal architecture for keeping that capacity subordinate to the state.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->",
    canon:"Large private spacecraft fleets, their near-naval capability, merchant-marine political language, continuing public sovereignty, and denial of a private right to make war are direct authorial direction. The 1985 licensed-carrier system, 1993 charter, reserve and requisition relationship, defensive-weapons rules, reserve commissions, compatible communications, government carriage, and conversion obligations are incorporated reconstruction. A formal service title, general statute, crew commission system, weapons rules, fleet register, activation process, and named armed corporate ship remain open.",
    sections:[
      {id:"origins",title:"Origins",html:`<p>The <a href='commercial-orbital-transportation-act.html'>Commercial Orbital Transportation Act of 1985</a> permitted licensed operators to acquire complete government-derived spacecraft. The Aerospace Force retained range, traffic, docking, rescue, strategic-payload, and emergency powers.</p><p>Frontier's 1993 <a href='national-orbital-carrier.html'>National Orbital Carrier</a> charter formalized a public-service relationship in which private capacity could serve routine markets while remaining available for national use.${americanOrbitalDoctrineCanon}</p>`},
      {id:"fleet",title:"Commercial fleet",html:"<p>The merchant fleet includes crew transports, automatic freighters, tankers, tugs, service craft, construction vehicles, and later interplanetary ships. Owners and operators may differ from the company that designed or built a vehicle. A carrier may own some spacecraft, lease others, and buy tug, depot, or yard services from specialist companies.</p><p>Frontier dominates much human transport, but specialist carriers, contractors, station operators, and industrial consortia retain vessels and chartered capacity. The resulting system is larger than a register of passenger craft: it includes the vehicles and crews that keep stations supplied, move propellant, service satellites, assemble structures, and sustain lunar and Mars routes.</p>"},
      {id:"obligations",title:"Public obligations",html:"<div class='table-wrap'><table class='wiki-table'><thead><tr><th>Obligation or feature</th><th>Purpose</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Licensing and inspection</td><td>Traffic safety, docking compatibility, crew qualification, and vehicle certification</td></tr><tr><td>Government carriage</td><td>Routine movement of personnel, mail, protected cargo, and agency equipment</td></tr><tr><td>Emergency requisition</td><td>Temporary public control during war, rescue, blockade, or strategic emergency</td></tr><tr><td>Conversion clauses</td><td>Design provisions for military communications, stores, defensive systems, or specialized payloads</td></tr><tr><td>Reserve personnel</td><td>Civil pilots and specialists available under military authority</td></tr><tr><td>Interoperability</td><td>Encrypted communications, navigation, identification, docking, and traffic-control compatibility</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>The precise statutory language and the extent to which each condition applies outside the National Orbital Carrier charter remain open.</p>"},
      {id:"defense",title:"Defensive armament",html:`<p>Commercial vessels may carry systems intended to protect crew, passengers, cargo, or stations. Candidate functions include debris interception, missile warning, terminal defense, sensor protection, and safe disengagement. Weapons custody and authorization remain subject to federal law and orbital agreements.</p><p>The same hardware can have ambiguous uses. A high-power sensor, maneuvering interceptor, close-defense gun, or hardened communication set may protect against debris and attack while also increasing a ship's military value. Licensing therefore turns on custody, software, rules of use, inspection, and the vessel's charter rather than on a simple division between armed and unarmed hulls.</p><p>Defensive armament does not give a corporation a general right to conduct offensive war. The unresolved boundary lies in how a merchant vessel acts during rescue, piracy, sabotage, foreign seizure, or wartime activation.</p>`},
      {id:"personnel",title:"Crews and reserve status",html:"<p>Commercial pilots, engineers, traffic officers, medical personnel, and technical crews can maintain reserve commissions or mobilization qualifications. Training and communications standards allow a vessel to enter national service without replacing its complete crew. Familiarity with a particular hull may be more valuable than substituting a wholly military complement at short notice.</p><p>Reserve status also gives the state a trained pool larger than its standing Aerospace Force. It does not make every commercial employee a service member in peacetime. The balance between corporate command, master or pilot authority, Aerospace Force orders, civilian labor rights, and individual mobilization obligations remains open.</p>"},
      {id:"mobilization",title:"Requisition and wartime service",html:"<p>Emergency authority can redirect traffic, seize priority cargo space, place a craft under military control, or requisition the whole vessel. Ships built with public subsidy may carry stronger conversion and availability clauses. Mobilization can therefore occur in degrees: priority carriage and routing precede full takeover, while an immediate rescue order need not create the same command relationship as war service.</p><p>The system resembles merchant shipping, airline reserve fleets, and armed auxiliaries more closely than a private army. Corporate ownership can persist while mission control, weapons authority, and strategic routing pass temporarily to the government. Military-compatible docking, identification, communications, stores, and refueling fittings reduce the time required for that transfer.</p>"},
      {id:"politics",title:"Political language",html:"<p>Supporters describe merchant crews as successors to merchant mariners, reserve aviators, privateers, and citizen soldiers. The preferred argument is that Americans conducting commerce beyond Earth should not be helpless and should remain available to national service. This language makes a large private fleet legible as a civic reserve rather than an alien transfer of sovereign power.</p><p>Critics regard the same system as corporate military capacity under a reassuring historical label. They point to subsidized construction, reserve crews, protected communications, conversion clauses, and defensive weapons as capabilities approaching those of an auxiliary navy. This disagreement becomes sharper as private ships grow larger and travel farther from immediate government support.</p><p>Both descriptions accept the same institutional overlap. The dispute is whether public control in an emergency is strong enough to keep commercial coercive capacity subordinate to law.</p>"},
      {id:"names",title:"Ship names and naval claims",html:"<p>Use of traditional naval and privateering names can provoke disputes with the United States Navy. A corporation may invoke merchant service, the Revolution, or citizen defense; the Navy may regard the same name as part of its institutional inheritance.</p><p><em>Bonhomme Richard</em> appears in planning discussion as a politically evocative candidate for a large corporate ship. Its historical association with John Paul Jones gives it exactly the disputed merchant-and-warship symbolism the system encourages, but it is not assigned to a fixed hull in the accepted reference.</p>"},
      {id:"status",title:"Status in 2012",html:"<p>Merchant-aerospace reserve and requisition principles are established, principally through Frontier's charter and the wider licensing system. By 5 January 2012 private vehicles provide much of the country's routine access to orbit and support a growing interplanetary economy, while federal authorities retain strategic traffic, military command, licensing, rescue, and emergency powers.</p><p>The name and legal organization of any permanent auxiliary service, its command structure, complete armed-vessel register, and wartime history remain open. No corporate fleet is established as possessing an independent right to make war.</p>"}
    ],
    related:[...americanOrbitalDoctrineRelated,{href:"national-orbital-carrier.html",kicker:"Strategic charter",label:"National Orbital Carrier"},{href:"commercial-orbital-transportation-act.html",kicker:"Licensing basis",label:"Commercial Orbital Transportation Act"},{href:"frontier-orbital-lines.html",kicker:"Principal carrier",label:"Frontier Orbital Lines"},{href:"commercial-spacecraft-procurement-united-states.html",kicker:"Vehicle acquisition",label:"Commercial Spacecraft Procurement in the United States"},{href:"american-orbital-carrier-requirement.html",kicker:"Military capital-spacecraft program",label:"American Orbital-Carrier Requirement"}],
    facts:[["System opened","1985"],["Principal charter","National Orbital Carrier · 1993"],["Character","Commercial fleet with public-service obligations"],["Government powers","Licensing · traffic control · rescue · requisition"],["Military integration","Reserve crews · compatible systems · conversion clauses"],["Defensive armament","Permitted in principle; rules open"],["Private right to wage war","Not established"],["Named armed corporate ship","Open"],["Article horizon","5 January 2012"]]
  }),

  "pilgrim-mars-flight-body": americanOrbitalDoctrineArticle({
    title:"Pilgrim Mars flight body",
    eyebrow:"Project Pilgrim · integrated atmospheric vehicle · 1980s",
    infoboxKicker:"Unflown American Mars landing vehicle",
    lead:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-p7r3x writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T09:36:00-06:00 -->The newest authorial correction directly requires Pilgrim's atmospheric element to aerobrake and land horizontally on Mars, without requiring the same craft to launch through Earth's atmosphere; the 1990 crash prevents the federal expedition and passes useful work into the corporate successor.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='a'>[a]</sup> Separation from an orbit-only transfer ship, lifting-body geometry, cross-range control, supersonic retropropulsion, powered approach, wheels, and the exact commercial lineage are incorporated engineering realization.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->",
    canon:"Pilgrim's horizontal Mars landing, aerobraking, freedom from Earth-launch requirements, prevention of the federal expedition by the 1990 crash, and survival of useful work into corporate hands are direct authorial direction. The distinct flight body, orbit-only transfer ship, lifting-body form, ventral protection, cross-range control, supersonic retropropulsion, powered approach, wheeled touchdown, testing chronology, and exact Marsplane lineage are incorporated reconstruction. Name, manufacturer, dimensions, mass, crew, engines, runway requirement, ascent method, test articles, and disposition remain open.",
    sections:[
      {id:"system",title:"Place in Project Pilgrim",html:`<p><a href='project-pilgrim.html'>Project Pilgrim</a> was a transportation system rather than one vehicle intended to perform every phase. The XO-100 transfer ship carried long-duration habitation, interplanetary propulsion, shielding, stores, power, and communications. Sending that complete structure through entry and back out of Martian gravity would impose a large mass penalty.</p><p>The flight body carried the atmospheric, landing, and immediate surface functions. It remained an integrated mission element even though it was structurally distinct from the transfer ship.${americanOrbitalDoctrineCanon}</p>`},
      {id:"configuration",title:"Configuration",html:"<p>The vehicle used a lifting-body form with a recognizable nose, broad lifting shoulders, a flat protected belly, dorsal crew or command region, aft and ventral landing propulsion, and heavy wheeled landing gear. It did not carry the air-breathing ascent machinery, Earth-reentry protection, or runway-launch structure of the first O-series spaceplanes.</p><p>Exact shape, control surfaces, pressure volume, payload bay, landing-gear arrangement, and relationship to the ascent vehicle remain open.</p>"},
      {id:"sequence",title:"Planned entry and landing sequence",html:"<div class='table-wrap'><table class='wiki-table'><thead><tr><th>Phase</th><th>Function</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Separation</td><td>Departure from the Pilgrim transfer system before atmospheric entry</td></tr><tr><td>Aerodynamic entry</td><td>Ventral thermal protection absorbs entry heating and the body sheds velocity</td></tr><tr><td>Cross-range maneuver</td><td>Lift and attitude control adjust the landing footprint</td></tr><tr><td>Supersonic retropropulsion</td><td>Engines operate during the high-speed terminal phase where the thin atmosphere is insufficient</td></tr><tr><td>Powered approach</td><td>The craft remains nose-forward while landing engines reduce horizontal and vertical velocity</td></tr><tr><td>Touchdown</td><td>Landing gear supports a horizontal wheeled arrival on a prepared lane or broad graded surface</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},
      {id:"control",title:"Control and crew orientation",html:`<p>The flight body preserved fixed forward, aft, dorsal, ventral, port, and starboard directions. Its cockpit or command spaces had an unambiguous local vertical. Entry guidance combined automated thermal and trajectory limits with pilot supervision inside a calculated corridor.</p><p>The configuration continued the <a href='american-spacecraft-design.html'>American aircraft-derived design grammar</a> while accepting that Mars landing required rockets as well as aerodynamic lift.</p>`},
      {id:"landing",title:"Landing site requirements",html:"<p>A horizontal landing required surveyed terrain, navigation aids, obstacle clearance, a graded lane or broad landing area, emergency dispersal, and surface support. The flight body could retain cross-range and a stable ground stance, but wheels and landing structure consumed mass.</p><p>Exact runway length, preparation machinery, braking method, wheel loading, abort sites, and tolerance for unimproved terrain remain open.</p>"},
      {id:"ascent",title:"Surface and ascent arrangements",html:"<p>The accepted reference does not determine whether the same body returned to orbit, carried a separable ascent stage, or served only as a lander and surface aircraft. Propellant production, ascent rendezvous, surface endurance, cargo unloading, and reusability therefore remain open.</p>"},
      {id:"testing",title:"Development and testing",html:"<p>Mars-entry and lifting-body work proceeded during the 1983–87 development period while the transfer ship was assembled in orbit. The 1988–89 shakedown qualified the interplanetary system short of a Mars expedition.</p><p>The current record does not identify complete flight bodies, atmospheric test articles, drop vehicles, or the disposition of surviving hardware.</p>"},
      {id:"inheritance",title:"Commercial inheritance",html:`<p>The 1990 crisis prevented the federal Mars flight. Personnel, simulations, entry research, structures, and other usable work entered the commercial successor system. Frontier and the American Mars consortium prepared a Mars-specific <a href='american-marsplane.html'>Marsplane</a> near Columbia in 1998 for the 1999 corporate landing.</p><p>The later Marsplane is a descendant, not evidence that the federal Pilgrim body itself landed on Mars.</p>`}
    ],
    related:[...americanOrbitalDoctrineRelated,{href:"project-pilgrim.html",kicker:"Parent program",label:"Project Pilgrim"},{href:"american-marsplane.html",kicker:"Commercial successor",label:"American Marsplane"},{href:"american-corporate-mars-program.html",kicker:"Post-1990 program",label:"American Corporate Mars Program"},{href:"liberty-yard.html",kicker:"Transfer-ship assembly site",label:"Liberty Yard"}],
    facts:[["Parent program","XO-100 / O-100 Pilgrim"],["Type","Integrated Mars atmospheric flight body"],["Configuration","Lifting body"],["Entry","Aerodynamic with cross-range control"],["Terminal descent","Supersonic retropropulsion and powered approach"],["Landing","Horizontal · wheeled"],["Federal Mars flight","Prevented in 1990"],["Commercial successor","American Marsplane"],["Detailed specifications","Open"]]
  })
});

const appendOrbitalDoctrineRelated = (id, items) => {
  const article = window.deepArticles[id];
  if (!article) return;
  article.related = article.related || [];
  for (const item of items) {
    if (!article.related.some(existing => existing.href === item.href)) article.related.push(item);
  }
};

